content:
{
  "summary": "This computer has two ways for other people to log in remotely, TightVNC and Remote Desktop, and both are open to the network while the Windows Firewall is off for the Public profile (the setting used on networks like cafés or hotels). Together these make the computer much easier to reach from other machines than it should be. First, turn the Public firewall back on. Then decide whether you actually use TightVNC and Remote Desktop, and confirm you recognize the extra administrator account.",
  "groups": [
    {
      "priority": 1,
      "title": "Firewall off on public networks, with remote-login services open",
      "finding_ids": [
        "FW-PROFILE-OFF:d0633f5d"
      ],
      "explanation": "The Windows Firewall is turned off for the Public profile. When you join a network Windows treats as public, nothing blocks other devices from reaching the programs listening on this computer. Here that includes TightVNC (port 5900) and Remote Desktop (port 3389). This is the most urgent item because it makes every other remote-access finding more serious. The report shows how to turn it back on."
    },
    {
      "priority": 2,
      "title": "TightVNC remote-control software is installed and accepting connections",
      "finding_ids": [
        "PORT-REMOTE-SERVICE:7cb92661",
        "REMOTE-ACCESS-TOOL:cb90e00a"
      ],
      "explanation": "TightVNC (C:\\Program Files\\TightVNC\\tvnserver.exe) is running and listening on port 5900 on all network connections. It lets someone control this computer from elsewhere. If you or someone helping you set it up on purpose, it may be fine, though it is still exposed while the firewall is off. If you don't recognize it, treat it as serious, because it could be how someone is getting in. The report's fix sections explain your options."
    },
    {
      "priority": 3,
      "title": "Remote Desktop is on and doesn't require sign-in first (no NLA)",
      "finding_ids": [
        "RDP-NLA-OFF:8e943d20",
        "RDP-ENABLED:62fe1d90",
        "PORT-REMOTE-SERVICE:b128d5fc"
      ],
      "explanation": "Remote Desktop is turned on and listening on port 3389. It is one of the most frequently attacked parts of Windows. It is also set up without Network Level Authentication, so anyone who can reach it gets to the Windows login screen before proving who they are. If you don't use Remote Desktop, turning it off removes all three findings at once. If you do use it, the report shows how to require NLA."
    },
    {
      "priority": 4,
      "title": "An extra administrator account to confirm",
      "finding_ids": [
        "ACCT-EXTRA-ADMIN:fde0b291"
      ],
      "explanation": "The account <computer>\\<account-2> has full administrator rights. Having a separate admin account is often normal and good practice. Just check that you know who created it and why. An admin account you don't recognize can be a sign of a break-in, which matters more here because remote-login services are exposed."
    }
  ],
  "severity_notes": []
}
